      en: "The Burica Peninsula is the southernmost headland shared between Costa Rica and Panama. Its topography is defined by low but very steep hills and deeply incised river valleys that empty directly into the Pacific. This is one of the most remote and inaccessible regions in the country; with almost no roads, transport often relies on boats or horseback. The landscape is wild and rugged, with sea cliffs and secluded beaches that are often backed by dense jungle. It is a frontier region where traditional fishing and subsistence farming remain the primary ways of life. The isolation has preserved a unique and undisturbed coastal ecosystem. Geography K8 - Border regions and geographic isolation."
